2181-8. Command descriptions
2182-Commands are issued to the device by loading the pertinent registers in the command block with the needed
2183-parameters, and then writing the command code to the Command register.
2184-Upon receipt of a command, the device sets the BSY bit or the DRQ bit within 400 ns. Following the setting
2185-of BSY bit equal to one or equal to zero and DRQ bit equal to one, the status presented by the device
2186-depends on the type of command: PIO data in, PIO data out, non-data transfer or DMA. See the individual
2187-command descriptions and clause 9 for the protocol followed by each command and command type.
2188-
2189:     NOTE 15 - Some older host implementations may require the BSY bit being set to zero and the DRQ
2190-     bit equal to one in the Status register within 700 ns of receiving some PIO data out commands.
2191:     NOTE 16 - For the power mode related commands, it is recommended that the host utilize E0h
2192-     through E3h, E5h and E6h command values. While command values 94h through 99h command
2193-     values are valid, they should be considered obsolete and may be removed in future versions of this
2194-     standard.
2195-In table 10, the "proto" column codes represent the command protocol used:
2196- DM - A DMA command;
2197- ND - A non data command;
2198- PI - A PIO data in command;
--
2201-The "typ" column codes represent the command type:
2202- O - Optional--the implementation of this command is optional;
2203- M - Mandatory--all ATA devices shall implement this command;
2204- R - Reserved for use in future ATA standards;
2205- V - Vendor specific implementation.
