   711: 3.1.4 Command acceptance: A command is considered accepted whenever the host writes to the
   712: Command Register and the device currently selected has its BSY bit equal to zero. An exception exists for
   713: the EXECUTE DIAGNOSTIC command (see the description of the EXECUTE DIAGNOSTIC command).
   714: 
   715:                                                  1
   716: X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
   717: 
   718: 3.1.5 CHS (Cylinder-head-sector): This term defines the addressing of the device as being by cylinder
   719: number, head number and sector number.
   720: 
   721: 3.1.6 Data block: This term describes a unit of data words transferred using PIO data transfer. A data
   722: block is transferred between the host and the device as a complete unit. A data block is a sector, except for
   723: data blocks of a READ MULTIPLE, WRITE MULTIPLE, READ LONG and WRITE LONG commands. In the
   724: cases of READ MULTIPLE and WRITE MULTIPLE commands, the size of the data block may be changed in
   725: multiples of sectors by the SET MULTIPLE MODE command. In the cases of READ LONG and WRITE
   726: LONG, the size of the data block is a sector plus a vendor specific number of bytes. The default length of
   727: the vendor specific bytes associate with the READ LONG and WRITE LONG commands is four bytes, but
   728: may be changed by use of the SET FEATURES command.
   729: 
   730: 3.1.7 Device: Device is a storage peripheral. Traditionally, a device on the ATA interface has been a hard
   731: disk drive, but any form of storage device may be placed on the ATA interface provided it adheres to this
   732: standard.
   733: 
   734: 3.1.8 Device selection: A device is selected when the DEV bit of the Drive/Head register is equal to the
   735: device number assigned to the device by means of a Device 0/Device 1 jumper or switch, or use of the
   736: CSEL signal.
   737: 
   738: 3.1.9 DMA (Direct memory access): A means of data transfer between device and host memory without
   739: processor intervention.
   740: 
   741: 3.1.10 LBA (Logical block address): This term defines the addressing of the device as being by the linear
   742: mapping of sectors.
   743: 
   744: 3.1.11 Master: Previous to this standard, Device 0 has also been referred to as the master. Throughout this
   745: document the term Device 0 shall be used.
   746: 
   747: 3.1.12 Optional: This term describes features which are not required by the standard. However, if any
   748: optional feature defined by the standard is implemented, it shall be done in the way defined by the standard.
   749: Describing a feature as optional in the text is done to assist the reader.
   750: 
   751: 3.1.13 PIO (Programmed input/output): A means of accessing device registers. PIO is also used to
   752: describe one form of data transfers. PIO data transfers are performed by the host processor utilizing PIO
   753: register accesses to the Data register.
   754: 
   755: 3.1.14 Reserved: Reserved bits, bytes, words, fields and code values are set aside for future
   756: standardization. Their use and interpretation may be specified by future xtensions to this or other standards.
   757: A reserved bit, byte, word or field all e set to zero, or in accordance with a future extension to this standard.
   758: The recipient shall not check reserved bits, bytes, words or fields. Receipt of reserved code values in
   759: defined fields shall be treated as an error.
   760: 
   761: 3.1.15 Sector: A uniquely addressable set of 256 words (512 bytes).
   762: 
   763: 3.1.16 Slave: Previous to this standard, Device 1 has also been referred to as the slave. Throughout this
   764: document the term Device 1 shall be used.
   765: 
   766: 3.1.17 Unrecoverable error: An unrecoverable error is defined as having occurred at any point when the
   993: 
   994:     VIH             Voltage Input High                      2,0 V d.c.
   995: 
   996:     VIL             Voltage Input Low                                   0,8 V d.c.
   997: 
   998:     VOH             Voltage Output High (IOH = -400 uA) 2,4 Vd.c.
   999: 
  1000:     VOL             Voltage Output Low (IOL = 12ma)                     0,5 V d.c.
