  1219: 5.2 Signal descriptions
  1220: 
  1221: 5.2.1 CS0- (CHIP SELECT 0)
  1222: 
  1223: This is the chip select signal from the host used to select the Command Block Registers. See table 7.
  1224:      NOTE 3 - This signal has also been known in the industry as CS1FX-.
  1225: 
  1226: 5.2.2 CS1- (CHIP SELECT 1)
  1227: 
  1228: This is the chip select signal from the host used to select the Control Block Registers. See table 7.
  1229:           NOTE 4 - This signal has also been known in the industry as CS3FX-.
  1230: 
  1231: 5.2.3 DA2, DA1, and DA0 D( EVICE ADDRESS)
  1232: 
  1233: This is the 3-bit binary coded address asserted by the host to access a register or data port in the device.
  1234: See table 7.
  1235: 5.2.4 DASP- (Device active, device 1 present)
  1236: This is a time-multiplexed signal which indicates that a device is active, or that Device 1 is present. This
  1237: signal shall be an open collector output and each device shall have a 10K ohm pull-up resistor.
  1238: During power on initialization or after RESET- is negated, DASP- shall be deasserted by both Device 0 and
  1239: Device 1 within 1 ms, and then Device 1 shall assert DASP- within 400 ms, to indicate that Device 1 is
  1240: present.
  1241: Device 0 shall allow up to 450 ms for Device 1 to assert DASP-.
  1242: 
  1243: 10
  1244:                                                                                                                X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1245: 
  1246: DASP- shall be negated following acceptance of a command by Device 1 or after 31 s, whichever comes
  1247: first.
  1248: 
  1249: Any time after negation of DASP-, either device may assert DASP- to indicate that a device is active.
  1250: 
  1251: If the host connects to the DASP- signal for the illumination of an LED or for any other purpose, the host shall
  1252: ensure that the signal level seen on the ATA interface for DASP- shall maintain Voh and Vol compatibility,
  1253: given the Ioh and Iol requirements of the DASP- device drivers.
  1254: 
  1255: 5.2.5 DD0-DD15 (Device Data)
  1256: 
  1257: This is an 8- or 16-bit bi-directional data interface between the host and the device. The lower 8 bits are
  1258: used for 8-bit transfers e.g., registers, vendor specific bytes associated with the READ LONG and WRITE
  1259: LONG commands and, if the device supports the Features register capability to enable 8-bit-only data
  1260: transfers (see 8.24, Set features).
  1261: 
  1262: 5.2.6 DIOR- (Device I/O read)
  1454: direction of commands previously transferred from the host. The device performs all of the operations
  1455: necessary to properly write data to, or read data from, the media. Data read from the media is stored in the
  1456: device's buffer pending transfer to the host memory and data is transferred from the host memory to the
  1457: device's buffer to be written to the media.
  1458: The devices using this interface shall be programmed by the host computer to perform commands and return
  1459: status to the host at command completion. When two devices are daisy chained on the interface, commands
  1460: are written in parallel to both devices, and for all except the Execute Diagnostics command, only the selected
  1461: device executes the command. On an Execute Diagnostics command addressed to Device 0, both devices
  1462: shall execute the command, and Device 1 shall post its status to Device 0 via PDIAG-.
  1463: Devices are selected by the DEV bit in the Device/Head register (see 6.2.7). When the DEV bit is equal to
  1464: zero, Device 0 is selected. When the DEV bit is equal to one, Device 1 is selected. When devices are daisy
  1465: chained, one shall be set as Device 0 and the other as Device 1. When a single device is attached to the
  1466: interface it shall be set as Device 0.
  1467: 
  1468: 6.2 I/O register descriptions
  1469: 
  1470: Communication to or from the device is through an I/O Register that routes the input or output data to or
  1471: from registers addressed by the signals from the host (CS0-, CS1-, DA2, DA1, DA0, DIOR- and DIOW-).
  1472: The Command Block Registers are used for sending commands to the device or posting status from the
  1473: device. The Control Block Registers are used for device control and to post alternate status.
  1474: Table 7 lists these registers and the addresses that select them.
  1475: 
  1476:                                                                                                                                               15
  1477: X3T10/0948D Revision 4c
  1478: 
  1479:                          Table 7 - I/O Port functions and selection addresses
  1480: 
  1481:                    Addresses                                                Functions
  1482: 
  1483:     CS0-     CS1-        DA2 DA1 DA0                              READ (DIOR-)         WRITE (DIOW-)
  1484: 
  1485:     N     N              x       x      x                 Data bus high imped          Not Used
  1486: 
  1487:                                                                        Control block registers
  1488: 
  1489:     N     A              0       x      x                 Data bus high imped          Not Used
  1490: 
  1491:     N     A              1       0      x                 Data bus high imped          Not Used
  1492: 
  1493:     N     A              1       1      0                 Alternate status             Device control
  1494: 
  1495:     N     A              1       1      1                 (Note 1)                     Not Used
  1496: 
  1497:                                                                        Command block registers
  1498: 
  1499:     A     N              0       0      0                 Data                         Data
  1500: 
  1501:     A     N              0       0      1                 Error                        Features
  1502: 
  1503:     A     N              0       1      0                 Sector Count                 Sector Count
  1504: 
